ntdll: a fault that names nothing should at least name where it came from.

`patches/wine/0043` made an unrepaired fault say *what* was touched:

    virtual:virtual_handle_fault unrepaired write fault at 0x100, no view covers it

That was enough for `0044`, where the address itself was the answer -- a stale
ntdll base plus `0x3c` named a relocated module. It is not enough for the one
Requiem is producing now. `0x100` is a null pointer plus an offset, which says
only "somebody dereferenced NULL"; every field of every structure in the process
is a candidate, and the address cannot narrow it further.

**Measured 2026-08-16: 92 faults from a single thread, 87 of the last 200 lines
of the session**, all at `0x100`, starting the instant the title's upscaler stack
finishes loading -- FidelityFX, XeSS with frame generation, and NVIDIA
Streamline, each probing for a vendor extension that correctly is not there.
Three candidate culprits, one address, and no way to tell them apart. Disabling
upscaling and frame generation in the game's own options did not stop it, which
kills the obvious guess and leaves nothing else to reason from.

So print the faulting instruction. `rec->ExceptionAddress` is the natural place
and it is empty here: `segv_handler` zeroes the record, fills in only the fault
kind and the fault address, and `setup_exception` sets the PC *after*
`virtual_handle_fault` has already run and returned (`signal_arm64.c:782`
against `:1126`). One assignment moved earlier gives the fault handler the same
value the exception would have carried, and `setup_exception` then writes the
identical value over it, so nothing downstream can observe the difference.

The result is one line that a reader can act on, because Vessel's default log
already carries `loaddll` at warning:

    unrepaired write fault at 0x100 from 0x6ff3ab1234, no view covers it
    ...
    Loaded L"D:\Games\...\libxess_fg.dll" at 0000006FF3AB0000: native

Subtract, and the module and its offset fall out. That is the difference between
"something wrote through NULL" and knowing which of three upscalers did it --
and, on a stripped third-party DLL, an offset is still what a disassembler needs.

ARM64 only, deliberately. This is the architecture the guest faults on, the other
three handlers are untouched, and a change that has to be right on four
architectures to be tested on one is a change that gets reverted.
Diagnostic-only: no behaviour, one `%p` on a path that already formats a string
and is already rate-limited by the fault itself.

diff --git a/dlls/ntdll/unix/signal_arm64.c b/dlls/ntdll/unix/signal_arm64.c
--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/signal_arm64.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/signal_arm64.c
@@ -1120,8 +1120,17 @@
     rec.NumberParameters = 2;
     if ((esr & 0xf0000000) == 0x80000000) rec.ExceptionInformation[0] = EXCEPTION_EXECUTE_FAULT;
     else if (esr & 0x40) rec.ExceptionInformation[0] = EXCEPTION_WRITE_FAULT;
     else rec.ExceptionInformation[0] = EXCEPTION_READ_FAULT;
     rec.ExceptionInformation[1] = (ULONG_PTR)siginfo->si_addr;
+    /* Vessel: the faulting PC, before the handler that wants to report it runs.
+     *
+     * setup_exception assigns exactly this at :782, but only after
+     * virtual_handle_fault has already decided and logged -- so the one report
+     * that fires for an unrepairable fault had a zero here and could name only
+     * the address touched. For a null-plus-offset fault that is no information
+     * at all. Overwritten below with the same value, so this is visibility and
+     * not a change. */
+    rec.ExceptionAddress = (void *)PC_sig(context);
     if (!virtual_handle_fault( &rec, (void *)SP_sig(context) )) return;
     if (handle_syscall_fault( context, &rec )) return;
     setup_exception( context, &rec );
diff --git a/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c b/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
@@ -5074,8 +5074,13 @@
     if (ret == STATUS_ACCESS_VIOLATION)
     {
         struct file_view *view = find_view( addr, 0 );
         const char *how = (err == EXCEPTION_WRITE_FAULT) ? "write" :
                           (err == EXCEPTION_EXECUTE_FAULT) ? "exec" : "read";
 
-        if (!view) ERR( "unrepaired %s fault at %p, no view covers it\n", how, addr );
+        /* The faulting PC as well as the faulting address, because an address
+         * like 0x100 is a null dereference and names nothing on its own. The
+         * PC minus the nearest `loaddll` base is the module and the offset, and
+         * that line is already in this log at its default level. */
+        if (!view) ERR( "unrepaired %s fault at %p from %p, no view covers it\n",
+                        how, addr, rec->ExceptionAddress );
         else if (WARN_ON(virtual))
